from lib import runs
import resnums as N
from resnums import f, sg, ref, P1, P2, P3, P4, TK

def summary_discussion(D):
    D.h1("X. SUMMARY AND DISCUSSION")
    D.p("Table XIII collects the main quantities from Sections V to IX in one place.")
    T = lambda m, a, b: P2[f"transfer_{m}_{a}_to_{b}"]["f1"]
    k10 = TK[TK.k == 10].groupby(["model", "target"])["diff"].mean()
    cm = N.CM[N.CM.mix != "nat_vs_swap"].groupby(["model", "ctx", "mix"])["rho_other"].mean()
    dd = lambda m, c: cm.loc[(m, c, "swapped")] - cm.loc[(m, c, "natural")]
    s12 = lambda m: [P3[f"sent_{m}_{c}_S1vsS2"]["rho"] for c in ("novpn", "vpn")]
    B = lambda m, c: P1[f"base_{m}_{c}"]["f1"]
    gp = lambda m, d: f"{sg(ref(m,d,'rho_gap'))} ({f(ref(m,d,'rho_gap','sd'))})"
    rows = [["Quantity", "Random Forest", "XGBoost", "Section"],
            ["Macro F1, Non-VPN / VPN", f"{f(B('RF','novpn'))} / {f(B('RF','vpn'))}", f"{f(B('XGB','novpn'))} / {f(B('XGB','vpn'))}", "V"],
            ["Primary cross-context ρ / RBO / Top-5", f"{f(P1['cross_RF']['rho'])} / {f(P1['cross_RF']['rbo'])} / {P1['cross_RF']['top5']}", f"{f(P1['cross_XGB']['rho'])} / {f(P1['cross_XGB']['rbo'])} / {P1['cross_XGB']['top5']}", "VI-B"],
            ["Half-split cross-context ρ (mean)", f(ref("RF", "random", "rho_cross")), f(ref("XGB", "random", "rho_cross")), "VI-C"],
            ["Δρ, random halves (SD)", gp("RF", "random"), gp("XGB", "random"), "VI-C"],
            ["Δρ, permutation null (SD)", gp("RF", "null"), gp("XGB", "null"), "VI-C"],
            ["Δρ, grouped ~10 / ~35 / ~140 per cluster", f"{sg(ref('RF','grouped10','rho_gap'))} / {sg(ref('RF','grouped35','rho_gap'))} / {sg(ref('RF','grouped','rho_gap'))}", f"{sg(ref('XGB','grouped10','rho_gap'))} / {sg(ref('XGB','grouped35','rho_gap'))} / {sg(ref('XGB','grouped','rho_gap'))}", "VI-C"],
            ["Class-mix swap: change in ρ with the other context (NV / VPN)", f"{sg(dd('RF','novpn'))} / {sg(dd('RF','vpn'))}", f"{sg(dd('XGB','novpn'))} / {sg(dd('XGB','vpn'))}", "VI-D"],
            ["Model transfer macro F1, Non-VPN→VPN / VPN→Non-VPN", f"{f(T('RF','novpn','vpn'))} / {f(T('RF','vpn','novpn'))}", f"{f(T('XGB','novpn','vpn'))} / {f(T('XGB','vpn','novpn'))}", "VIII-A"],
            ["Top-10 list transfer, own − transferred F1 (NV / VPN target)", f"{sg(k10[('RF','novpn')])} / {sg(k10[('RF','vpn')])}", f"{sg(k10[('XGB','novpn')])} / {sg(k10[('XGB','vpn')])}", "VIII-B"],
            ["Within-context ρ, S1 vs S2 encoding (NV / VPN)", f"{f(s12('RF')[0])} / {f(s12('RF')[1])}", f"{f(s12('XGB')[0])} / {f(s12('XGB')[1])}", "IX"]]
    D.table("Summary of the main results", [4300, 1950, 1950, 826], rows,
            note="Δρ is the mean over 11 repeats of within-context minus cross-context Spearman agreement; SD describes the spread over repeats. Positive transfer differences are a cost of using the other context's feature list.")
    D.h2("A. What the References Show")
    D.p("The answer to the question in the title depends on what cross-context agreement is compared with. Against models trained on random halves of one context, VPN and Non-VPN rankings disagree clearly more, and the permutation null shows that neither the half-split procedure nor the class-mix difference produces such a gap. Against models trained on groups of related flows, the excess disappears. The cross-context difference is real in the sense that it is not random noise, but it is of the same size as the difference between clusters of related flows within one context, our proxy for capture sessions. Without session identifiers or an independent second capture, the benchmark cannot show whether the VPN itself changes model reliance. A random within-context split, which is the usual way such references are built, would have suggested that it does.")
    D.h2("B. Accuracy and Correlation Do Not Guarantee Consistent Explanations")
    D.p("Predictive accuracy, whole-ranking correlation and agreement among the leading features are separate properties. A cross-context Spearman of about 0.8 coexists with three shared Top-5 features and an RBO of 0.58–0.75, and the leading features themselves change with the explanation method: the minimum IATs that rise most under SHAP move much less under permutation importance. Reporting performance, whole-ranking and top-weighted agreement, a within-context reference built from data that is as independent as possible, and a second importance measure gives a fuller picture than any one summary.")
    D.h2("C. Correlated Features and the Role of Grouping")
    D.p("Aggregation into semantic families narrows the gaps, but a family-level ranking measures something different from a feature ranking, and the family definitions decide which differences get hidden. Summing favours large families, per-member means and correlation-based clusters give larger gaps, and moving two correlated totals into the duration family changes which family leads. Family-level conclusions should be stated together with the grouping rule and the family sizes.")
    D.h2("D. Do Explanation Differences Matter?")
    D.p("The two consequence tests point in different directions. A trained model does not survive the change of context: macro F1 falls to 0.43–0.61. A SHAP-selected feature list mostly does: at k = 10 and 15 the transferred list loses at most about 0.013 macro F1, and for some targets it does better than the own list. Feature lists are coarse summaries that tolerate the ranking disagreement, while the models built on them are not. At k = 5, where the lists differ in two of five features, the cost can reach 0.05 for one model and context, so the conclusion holds only for moderately long lists.")
    D.h2("E. Implications for Explainable Network Security Systems")
    D.p("An explanation report for a traffic classifier should name the observation context, the fitted model, the attribution formulation, the encoding of undefined statistics and any aggregation rule. Consistency claims need a reference, and that reference should be built from data that does not share sessions with itself; a random split of correlated flows will overstate within-context agreement. Where an explanation drives a decision, such as pruning features, the decision should be checked on held-out data from the target context, and a deployed model should not be expected to transfer to a differently observed context because its explanations look similar.")

def threats(D):
    D.h1("XI. THREATS TO VALIDITY AND LIMITATIONS")
    D.lim("Session dependence and the missing group split.", "This is the most serious limitation, and it decides the main conclusion. The processed files carry no session or capture identifiers, so no reference in this paper is a true group split. Cluster-grouped halves are a proxy: fine clusters may still let related flows cross the split, and coarse clusters also separate regions of feature space that a session split would not. The cross-context gap shrinks steadily as grouping becomes stricter, so its size depends on an unknown session structure. Regenerating flows from the raw captures and splitting by capture file, or using a second independent capture of both contexts, would settle this.")
    D.lim("Benchmark age, reuse and construction.", "The ISCX VPN-nonVPN captures are several years old, come from one laboratory and one VPN service, and reflect the applications and protocols in use at the time. The dataset is among the most reused benchmarks in the field, and the released files cap BROWSING at 2,500 flows and Non-VPN P2P at 1,000 flows, so two class frequencies reflect the dataset's construction. We claim no dataset novelty and draw no conclusions about present-day applications or VPN technologies.")
    D.lim("Class distribution.", "The contexts have different class mixes. Class weighting, the class-mix swap, class-matched training with a same-size reference and the permutation null all leave the class-mix effect small, but the swapped training sets are limited to 3,400 flows and the matched sets reuse every Non-VPN MAIL flow.")
    D.lim("Correlated features and aggregation.", "How attribution is allocated depends on the model and the value function, and the drop-one and permutation checks show that part of the minimum-IAT shift is credit moving among partial substitutes. Summed family importance neither corrects for feature dependence nor provides an instance-level group attribution, and other groupings give other consistency patterns.")
    D.lim("Model, TreeSHAP and global aggregation.", "Only tree ensembles are explained, hyperparameters were not tuned, and only two TreeSHAP formulations were compared, the interventional one with a background of 200 training records. Importance is averaged over model outputs and explained records, so the results say nothing definite about consistency for particular classes or single flows.")
    D.lim("Consequence tests.", "The feature-list test uses five inner splits per context and one refit per feature set; its intervals hold the models fixed, and it is not an equivalence test. The model-transfer test uses one model per context.")
    D.lim("Representation.", "The data offer 23 time-related flow statistics and nothing else. Packet-size features and packet sequences, which modern encrypted-traffic classifiers rely on, are missing, and so are newer protocols such as TLS 1.3 and QUIC and VPN technologies such as WireGuard. Other treatments of undefined statistics, such as dropping them or imputation, were not evaluated, and some timestamp artefacts (negative minimum IATs) remain in the released files.")
    D.lim("Replication.", "Every conclusion concerns one dataset, one flow timeout and one feature representation. The repeated designs reuse one dataset and overlapping test records, and Random Forest half-models are explained on 800 test records each. None of this replaces replication on independent captures or modern traffic.")

def conclusion(D):
    D.h1("XII. CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK")
    D.p("We asked whether a classifier explains the same seven application categories with the same features when traffic is observed with and without a VPN, using the ISCX VPN-nonVPN benchmark and its 23 time-related flow features.")
    D.p(f"Both ensembles lose performance under VPN (XGBoost macro F1 {f(P1['base_XGB_novpn']['f1'],4)} to {f(P1['base_XGB_vpn']['f1'],4)}, Random Forest {f(P1['base_RF_novpn']['f1'],4)} to {f(P1['base_RF_vpn']['f1'],4)}), and a model trained in one context reaches only 0.43–0.61 macro F1 in the other. The primary SHAP rankings are related across contexts but not interchangeable: Spearman ρ is {f(P1['cross_RF']['rho'],2)} and {f(P1['cross_XGB']['rho'],2)}, RBO {f(P1['cross_RF']['rbo'],2)} and {f(P1['cross_XGB']['rbo'],2)}, and each model keeps three of its five leading features.")
    D.p(f"Whether this disagreement reflects the context depends on the reference. Against random halves of one context the within-minus-cross gap is {f(ref('XGB','random','rho_gap'),2)} for XGBoost and {f(ref('RF','random','rho_gap'),2)} for Random Forest over 11 repeats with redrawn partitions, and a permutation null that keeps the class mixes gives little or none ({sg(ref('XGB','null','rho_gap'))} and {sg(ref('RF','null','rho_gap'))}). Against halves that keep clusters of similar flows together, the gap shrinks to {f(ref('XGB','grouped10','rho_gap'),2)}–{f(ref('RF','grouped10','rho_gap'),2)} with small clusters and disappears with larger ones. Class mix explains little, and the most visible shift, the rise of the minimum IATs, is partly a redistribution of Shapley credit among correlated timing features. A SHAP-selected feature list transfers between contexts at a cost of at most about 0.013 macro F1 for lists of ten or more features.")
    D.p("On this benchmark, then, the cross-context differences in SHAP rankings cannot be distinguished from differences between groups of related flows within a context, and they should not be read as an effect of the VPN. Settling the question requires captures that keep session identifiers or a second independent capture of both contexts, more recent traffic, and class-specific explanations.")
